{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>You shall not murder, you shall not commit adultery, you shall not steal.</b> He said: Behold, I have commanded you to acknowledge that I am the Creator of all — in thought and in deed — and to honor the fathers because they participate in the formation. If so, guard yourself lest you spoil the work of My hands and shed the blood of the man whom I created for My honor and to acknowledge Me in all these things. <b>And you shall not commit adultery</b> with the wife of your fellow, for you thereby spoil the matter of the fathers' honor, denying the truth and acknowledging falsehood, since they will not know their father and will give their honor to another, as idolaters do: \"They say to a tree, 'You are my father'\" (Jeremiah 2:27), and they do not know the Father who created them — from where He is. And afterward He warned <b>you shall not steal</b> a person, for this too causes such a thing. And thus the order of the commandments in their severity and their punishment is thus: after idolatry, bloodshed, and afterward forbidden sexual relations, and after that He warned kidnapping and false testimony and robbery. And whoever does not covet will not cause harm to his fellow in the world. Behold, He completed everything that a person owes regarding his fellow. And afterward He will explain the ordinances in detail, for one who is liable to his fellow in judgment by means of the ordinances — if he does not covet and does not desire what is not his, he will pay what is upon him.\n\nAnd Rabbi Abraham ibn Ezra said (in his introduction to the Ten Commandments) that Scripture grasped the way of the world, saying first \"your fellow's house,\" for the prudent person first acquires a house for himself, and afterward takes a wife to bring her into his house, and afterward acquires a servant and maidservant. And in the repetition of the Torah he mentioned the wife first, for young men desire to take a wife first. Or because desire for a forbidden woman is the greatest among them. And behold, the Ten Commandments are five concerning the honor of the Creator and five for the benefit of man, for <b>Honor your father</b> is the honor of El, for the honor of the Creator He commanded to honor the father who participates in formation, and the remaining five are for man in his needs and his benefit.\n\nNow, in some of the commandments He mentioned their recompense, and in some He did not. For in the second commandment, <b>an El of zeal (קנא)</b>; in the third, <b>for He will not absolve (לא ינקה)</b>; in the fifth, <b>so that they may prolong</b>; and in the others He did not mention punishment or reward. And this is because the latter five commandments are for man's benefit, and behold, his reward is with him and his recompense is before him. But concerning idolatry, a warning of punishment is needed because of the great severity in it, and it concerns the honor of the Creator. And it appears to me that He said <b>an El of zeal</b> concerning <b>You shall not have</b>, and He said <b>and doing kindness</b> concerning <b>I am</b>, for the punishment comes upon the negative commandment, and the reward upon the positive commandment. And acceptance of the sovereignty of El and the warning against serving anything other than Him are one matter, and He completed the matter and afterward warned concerning the transgressor, and promised reward concerning the one who acts. And He warned concerning a false oath with the punishment <b>He will not absolve</b>. And He did not mention any recompense at all for the Sabbath, neither did He state for the one who profanes the Sabbath a punishment of excision or any other punishment besides it, nor did He mention reward for the one who keeps the Sabbath from profaning it — because the Sabbath is included in the two first commandments. For the one who keeps the Sabbath testifies to the act of Creation and acknowledges the commandment of <b>I am</b>, and the one who profanes it denies the act of Creation and acknowledges the eternity of the world (pre-existing matter), so as to deny the commandment of <b>I am</b>. Behold, it is included in <b>an El of zeal who visits</b>, and included in <b>and doing kindness to thousands</b>. And in the fifth commandment, concerning the honoring of fathers, He mentioned reward, for it is a positive commandment.\n\nAnd it appears from the writing on the tablets that the first five were on one tablet — they being the honor of the Creator, as I have mentioned — and the latter five on one tablet, so that there would be five corresponding to five, as in the matter mentioned in Sefer Yetzirah (1:3): \"In ten sefirot without substance, like the number of ten fingers, five corresponding to five, with a singular covenant set in the middle.\" And from this it will become clear to you why there were two, for up to <b>Honor your father</b> corresponds to the Written Torah, and from here onward corresponds to the Oral Torah. And it appears that our Sages, of blessed memory, alluded to this when they said (Tanhuma, Ekev 10): two tablets corresponding to heaven and earth, corresponding to bridegroom and bride, corresponding to two attendants, and corresponding to two worlds. And all this is one allusion, and the enlightened will understand the secret."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:12:07.535551736Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}